welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Alex Deb's  an associate professor of political  science and a research fellow at the  Macmillan Center his work focuses on the  causes of war nuclear proliferation and  democratization and his work has been  featured in numerous scholarly  publications today we'll talk with  Professor Debs  about the new book he co-wrote with Nuno  Montero titled nuclear politics the  strategic causes of proliferation  welcome professor Deb's good to be with  you well your book comes at the perfect  time especially with all of the news  about North Korea and Iran so let's get  right down to it tell us about your book  well what the book does is it oh it  looks at the history of nuclear  proliferation and considers the causes  of proliferation why our state's  interested in acquiring a nuclear weapon  and which states are successful in this  endeavor and so the book offers a theory  of the causes of proliferation and  reviews the diplomatic history between  the states that are considering  acquiring nuclear weapons and other  concerned States enemies and allies  alike okay and what led you to write the  book well if we look at other  explanations of nuclear proliferation  intuitively the security angle would be  the most compelling nuclear weapons are  after all weapons but people who have  taken that approach have only looked at  the interests of states that want to  acquire nuclear weapons and that's led  to an over prediction of proliferation  too many states would be interested in  getting them and too few get them and so  we were interested in understanding how  we can solve that puzzle and how we can  understand which states are successful  in acquiring nuclear weapons and in the  process of doing the research we amassed  a lot of information much of it is  available digitally  with for example the diplomatic archives  of the Foreign Relations of the United  States being available online and it  became clear to us that this needed to  be in the form of a book as opposed to  small articles okay and and how did you  do the research for it in addition to  what you just said yeah so we did the  research it was a dialogue between  theory and evidence and so we try to  break down the problem into small parts  so first looking at relations between  potential proliferators and their  enemies and understanding when states  are successful at getting weapons and  then adding a third another dimension  which is the role of allies in many  cases for example the u.s. is involved  in many security commitments and some  states even though they have security  commitments from the US have at various  points consider getting nuclear weapons  but once we have the baseline of states  without security allies we can  understand the effect of alliances and  that's how we broke down the theoretical  problem and in terms of the evidence as  I said there's a lot that's available  both digital digitally from the web but  also from archives and secondary sources  and so we analyze that data to try to  see what were States leaders thinking in  the pros and cons of getting nuclear  weapons and and then went back to the  blackboard and revised the theory  accordingly okay and despite what we  hear in the news today particularly  about North Korea not very many  countries have nuclear weapons why is  that well that's one of the puzzles that  motivated our theory and we seem to be  very concerned about proliferation but  that's one of the paradoxes of  proliferation that not many countries  have nuclear weapons so should we worry  about it should we not worry about it  they don't have them but do they want  them some definitely want them and  others probably not so much right so I  mean if we look back we're currently  worried about North Korea which has  proven its capability of having nuclear  weapons but if we rerun a few years back  before that it was Iran before that it  was Iraq so there were states that  expressed an interest in getting nuclear  weapons and often we talk of nuclear  weapons as the weapons of the  week so if a state would lose a  conventional war it would greatly  benefit from acquiring nuclear weapons  and so there is documented evidence of  state leaders being interested in  getting nuclear weapons but very few are  actually successful in acquiring them  and why is that so for us it's that  there are unique circumstances where a  state is both interested in getting  nuclear weapons and able to get them  so in short nuclear weapons or weapons  of the weak but that we can't get them  so the same reason that a state but why  can't they get them because of the  actions of other states around them the  enemies and the allies so if we it would  seem to me that because it's information  that already existed and is out there  it's so very difficult to figure out how  to build a nuclear weapon  hmm well so there's a technological  aspect so you need to clear certain  hurdles in rich in uranium having the  delivery capabilities and all these  technical problems and now certainly  that will mean that certain states that  otherwise would have their security  needs met by an alliance may say this  would be too costly and I won't consider  it but there are many states that do  consider it they do consider there to be  a significant security benefit but in  that case they can be either coerced or  reassured by allies that they actually  don't need nuclear weapons so if we look  at States for example like the Soviet  Union the Soviet Union had a clear  security need but it was able to acquire  nuclear weapons because the u.s.  preventive war plans that that  Washington was considering were not  workable the cost would have been too  high whereas you have weaker states like  Iran Iraq and Syria thought at very  responsive having nuclear weapons but  they were unable to reach to include  that threshold and so a state needs to  be strong enough that it can withstand  pressures by other state and and and get  me - when you say strong enough what do  you mean by that so here in terms of the  measures we're looking at conventional  capabilities so when we look at  conventional capabilities there's  various measures that are being used but  you can look at manpower the size of the  armament size of the military  and so states that have otherwise a very  small military may feel that if there is  a security need getting nuclear weapons  could be their trump card but by the  same token if they're relatively weak  and their enemies have more manpower  military power these enemies are more  successful at using coercive threats and  and preventing those states from getting  nuclear weapons okay except in the case  of North Korea for instance so let's  talk about that a little bit yes you  know in fact give can you give the  trajectory of North Korean and how it's  come to now have a nuclear weapon yes so  North Korea has a clear security needs  the Korean War in 1953 ended with an  armistice not a peace treaty and so  technically North Korea is still at war  with the United States during the Cold  War North Korea had an alliance with the  Soviet Union and China two states that  provided assistance during the Korean  War especially the Chinese but North  Korea came to doubt the reliability of  the of these alliances and in the 80s  both the Soviet Union and China wanted  to improve their relations with South  Korea and so and so for North Korea it  made sense for them to have their own  deterrent and also looking at their  trajectory both economically and  militarily  the South Koreans were growing relative  to the North Koreans and so it made  sense to have this ultimate trump card  so you can see a clear security need for  having nuclear weapons but they also had  the opportunity to have nuclear weapons  and this is in part because of accidents  of geography so Seoul is very close to  the North Korean border only 30 miles or  so from the border and that's meant that  any preventive war plans that the  Americans and the South Koreans  considered were not very workable it  would have led to very high casualties  in South Korea even with North Korean  conventional response and so Bill  Clinton considered preventive war plans  and tabled them the United States came  to an agreed framework with North Korea  where North Korea would freeze its  program in exchange for economic  inducements but in the late 90s and  early 2000s the US was worried that  North Korea was not following through on  its commitments and the North Koreans  were worried that the Americans were not  quick enough in providing the incentives  that they had promised and so in the  Bush administration that george w bush  administration the agreed framework fell  apart North Korea withdrew from the NPT  and made its first test into that 2006  and its second in 2009 okay so when we  look at North Korea basically the last  few decades have been a family-run  country yes and it seems to me contrary  to them not necessarily wanting  protection but wanting to to weld to  wield power over the rest of the world  by developing a nuclear weapon mm-hmm  how does that fit into your strategy  well I think so there's I mentioned that  the the perspective we follow is one of  security so as a state worried about its  security would there be an advantage in  having new nuclear weapons other  perspectives are focused on this  argument that's described as one of  prestige so for example states like  France had a certain perception of their  role in the world and having nuclear  weapons was an argument to pursue those  interests I have yet to find a state  that's not interested in prestige so so  any state would be interested in  prestige and so for us even though these  arguments and these perspective might be  useful what we wanted to do is restrict  ourselves to the security dimension and  see how much of the variation we could  explain in empirical patterns okay so  how many countries actually do have  nuclear weapons we're looking at nine  current nuclear powers the US Russia  England France China India Pakistan  Israel and North Korea very few actually  and and how many actually would want  them North Korea where does Iran fall so  Iran has had a nuclear program but now  has come to an agreement to freeze its  it's its capabilities and so  you could think there is an interest the  US intelligence services have all  concluded that the leadership has never  made a decision to acquire nuclear  weapons but obviously other states are  skeptical and so so there's an there's a  latent capability in Iran so ultimately  in your book what do you conclude so  what we conclude is to best understand  proliferation we need to think about the  security dimensions but not just looking  at states interested in getting nuclear  weapons but like looking at their  relationship with other states  potentially concerned so enemies and  allies and so if we only look at  relationship between enemies US nuclear  weapons or weapons of the weak but that  we can't get them so you need to have  enough power enough conventional power  to withstand pressures of your enemies  and acquire nuclear weapons so a state  like the Soviet Union was able to get  nuclear weapons but states like Iran  Iran Iraq and Syria have not been able  to now once we know this we can look at  the effect of alliances and here allies  can play two roles they can reassure  potential proliferators and they can  also coerce them and reminding them that  they shouldn't try to acquire nuclear  weapons  and so we can understand also how these  two tools are effective if you're  looking at relatively strong potential  proliferators assurances might be  sufficient to prevent them from getting  nuclear weapons and if you're looking at  relatively weak potential proliferators  then here a more coercive approach could  be successful reminding the ally that  without for example the u.s. security  guarantees these states would be too  vulnerable and they wouldn't be able to  acquire nuclear weapons okay I'm going  to put you on the spot here for a minute  and ask you what do you think the United  States could have done better or should  be doing to lessen the impact or the  situation that is going on with North  North Korea right now well I think as I  mentioned before the North Korean  situation is particularly tricky because  there is no good military option and  even folks within the Trump  administration  I've admitted as much by saying if we  look at the plans even with their  conventional capabilities they could  inflict very severe damage on our allies  and our troops stationed in the region  and so I think it is concerning to see  the administration escalating rhetoric  when in fact the the military options  are not very good so I think we might  look back and say in general the US has  been relatively good at preventing  proliferation there are some situations  where it's very hard to do it but by  escalating the risks and maybe running  the risk of drawing a wedge between the  US and its allies in the region that  could lead to further proliferation so  if South Korea and Japan come to doubt  our security guarantees they may decide  at some point that they are-- they need  their own deterrent and the u.s.  experience so far in the region there  has been one of success of reassuring  these allies that the US would be there  and would be a responsible player in  that region all right well it will be  interesting to see how it plays out and  the weeks ahead thank you so much for  being here with us today and sharing  some of your work thank you for more  information about Professor Deb's and  his research please visit our website at  macmillan report yale.edu be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale  [Music]

